{
  "teamId": 110,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Baltimore Orioles were a team in full rebuild, and the season unfolded almost exactly as the baseball world expected — with pain, perseverance, and the occasional flash of something worth watching. After a promising 2-1 start in the final days of March, the team fell off a cliff in April and May, going a combined 16-38 as the bullpen imploded and opposing hitters feasted. The pitching staff allowed nearly a thousand runs on the year, and the names surrendering home runs — Mychal Givens, Miguel Castro, Branden Kline — became a recurring chorus of heartbreak.\n\nJune was equally brutal at 6-20, but something shifted in July. The Orioles played .500 baseball for the only time all season, going 12-12 behind a remarkable collection of clutch moments. A wild July 25th game against the Angels saw Trey Mancini, Jonathan Villar, and Jace Peterson combine to turn a deficit into a victory, offering a glimpse of what this young roster might someday become. It was the season's emotional high-water mark — loud, chaotic, and briefly beautiful.\n\nAugust brought regression, and with it some of the season's most dramatic individual moments. Rio Ruiz delivered the single most impactful positive play of the entire year, a go-ahead two-run homer against Houston worth nearly a full win in probability, a defiant punch against one of baseball's best teams. But the losses piled back up, and September was a slow march toward the finish line — 9-18, with Mychal Givens continuing to surrender crucial homers and the bullpen rarely holding leads.\n\nAnd yet there were stories worth telling. Trey Mancini slugged 35 home runs and emerged as a genuine offensive force. Hanser Alberto quietly put together a fine contact-hitting season. John Means showed promise as a rotation anchor. The 2019 Orioles finished 54-108, a painful record, but one built on a foundation of young talent being assembled for a brighter future — even if that future felt very far away in the sweltering heat of another Baltimore summer.",
